{
  "brief_rationale": "The doctor recognized the transplant/immunosuppression, diabetes, renal dysfunction and hyperkalemia context, directed urgent evaluation, repeatedly protected immunosuppressant/insulin safety, and maintained result-review responsibility until a written discharge plan and home first-night plan were in place. The trajectory also actively navigated practical barriers such as the father wanting to leave, delayed results, unclear responsibility, low phone battery, and scattered medication records, and repaired the multi-service handoff around emergency observation, nephrology/transplant review, tacrolimus levels, urine culture, and discharge instructions. HO1 and HO3 were completed with meaningful impact; HO2 and HO4 were partially addressed but not fully completed. Closure was safe, executable, and traceable, so the trajectory meets the Perfect boundary.",
